---
title: 'CI Configuration File'
description: 'Configure Nx Cloud CI Pipeline Executions with the .nx/ci-config.yaml file'
filter: 'type:References'
---

Nx Cloud reads CI configuration from a `.nx/ci-config.yaml` file at the root of your workspace.
The file controls how a CI Pipeline Execution runs: task distribution, agent lifecycle, self-healing, and the environment forwarded to agents.
Every Nx Cloud command reads the file, so the configuration applies to the run whichever command starts it. To provision Workers, run [`nx-cloud start-workers`](/docs/reference/nx-cloud-cli#nx-cloud-start-workers).

```yaml
# .nx/ci-config.yaml
dte:
  distribute-on: 5 linux-large
lifecycle:
  stop-after:
    - build
    - test
    - e2e
```

Start the run by invoking the command once, early in your main job:

```shell
npx nx-cloud start-workers
```

The command takes no configuration flags. Everything lives in the file, so the same configuration is versioned with your workspace and can be overridden per environment.

{% aside type="caution" title="start-ci-run and .nx/ci-config.yaml cannot be combined" %}
`nx-cloud start-ci-run` does not read `.nx/ci-config.yaml`. If a config file is present, `start-ci-run` exits with an error and directs you to `start-workers`.
Choose one path: either the config file with `start-workers`, or CLI flags with `start-ci-run`.
For a mapping between the two, see the [Nx Cloud CLI reference](/docs/reference/nx-cloud-cli).
{% /aside %}

{% aside type="note" title="Runs without distribution use start-ci-run" %}
If you don't want distribution, use `npx nx-cloud start-ci-run --no-distribution` instead.
{% /aside %}

## File location

Nx Cloud reads the file from `.nx/ci-config.yaml`. A `.nx/ci-config.yml` extension also works.

## Top-level structure

The file has five top-level sections. All are optional, and each falls back to its defaults when omitted.

| Section     | Purpose                                                           |
| ----------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `lifecycle` | Stop conditions and stall detection for the run.                  |
| `ai`        | Self-healing CI configuration.                                    |
| `dte`       | Distributed task execution: how tasks are distributed to Workers. |
| `workers`   | Configuration for the Workers themselves.                         |
| `overrides` | Per-environment overrides keyed by `NX_CI_EXECUTION_ENV`.         |

For complete pipelines that pair this file with a provider workflow, see [setting up CI](/docs/getting-started/setup-ci) and [CI workflow examples](/docs/kb/ci-workflow-examples).

## `lifecycle`

Stop conditions and stall detection for the run group.

```yaml
# .nx/ci-config.yaml
lifecycle:
  fail-after: 1
  heartbeat: true
  stop-after:
    - build
    - test
    - e2e
```

### `lifecycle.fail-after`

Number of task failures allowed before the run group is marked failed.
`0` means never fail, so the run always finishes. `1` stops on the first failure.

Type: `integer`, minimum `0`. Default: `1`.

### `lifecycle.heartbeat`

When `true`, Nx Cloud monitors the run for stalled progress and marks it failed if the main job stops reporting in for too long.

Type: `boolean`. Default: `true`.

### `lifecycle.stop-after`

Targets that mark this CI run complete once each one has finished.
List every target you expect in the pipeline so the run does not end before all work is done.

Type: array of strings.

```yaml
# .nx/ci-config.yaml
lifecycle:
  stop-after:
    - lint
    - test
    - build
```

## `ai`

Self-healing CI configuration.
See [self-healing CI](/docs/features/ci-features/self-healing-ci) for how the fixes are generated and applied.

```yaml
# .nx/ci-config.yaml
ai:
  fix-tasks-patterns:
    - '*lint*'
    - '*test*'
  auto-apply-patterns:
    - '*lint*'
```

### `ai.fix-tasks-patterns`

Glob patterns of task IDs that self-healing CI is allowed to attempt fixes for.

Type: array of strings.

### `ai.auto-apply-patterns`

Glob patterns of task IDs whose fixes are applied automatically without explicit approval.

Type: array of strings.

## `dte`

Distributed task execution: how tasks are distributed to Workers.

```yaml
# .nx/ci-config.yaml
dte:
  enabled-by-default: true
  distribute-on: 5 linux-large
  assignment-rules: .nx/assignment-rules.yaml
```

### `dte.enabled-by-default`

When `true`, tasks in this run group are distributed across agents automatically.

Type: `boolean`. Default: `true`.

### `dte.distribute-on`

How many agents to use and which [launch templates](/docs/kb/launch-templates) to run them on.
Accepts a single launch-template string, or a map of changeset sizes to launch-template strings for [dynamic agent allocation](/docs/features/ci-features/distribute-task-execution#scale-workers-with-pr-size).

A launch-template string is one or more `<number> <template-name>` segments separated by commas.

```yaml
# .nx/ci-config.yaml
dte:
  distribute-on: 5 linux-large
```

```yaml
# .nx/ci-config.yaml
dte:
  distribute-on:
    small-changeset: 3 linux-medium
    medium-changeset: 6 linux-large
    large-changeset: 10 linux-large
```

The changeset map can also live in its own file.
Set `distribute-on` to that file's path and Nx Cloud reads the map from it.

```yaml
# .nx/ci-config.yaml
dte:
  distribute-on: .nx/workflows/distribution-config.yaml
```

To bring your own compute instead of using Workers, set `distribute-on: manual`.
See [bring your own compute](/docs/kb/bring-your-own-compute).

### `dte.main-job-worker`

Run distributed tasks on a persistent worker in the main CI job, sharing its checkout and resources without changing the remote worker count.
Omitted or `false` disables it; on Nx Cloud CI, `true` automatically starts it with the usual worker CPU and memory detection.
External CI requires explicit resources:

```yaml
# .nx/ci-config.yaml
dte:
  main-job-worker:
    cpu: 4
    memory: 12GiB
overrides:
  windows:
    dte:
      main-job-worker: false
```

`nx-cloud start-workers` (legacy alias: `start-nx-agents`) initializes both remote workers and the main-job worker; managed distributed jobs already invoke it, so no user command is needed there.
The override above disables the worker when `NX_CI_EXECUTION_ENV=windows`; assignment rules can select an enabled main-job worker with `agent: main`.

### `dte.scheduling-constraints`

Tasks that must never run on the same agent at the same time, for example because they share a port or a database.
Each entry selects tasks by `projects`, `targets`, or `configurations` globs, and `cannot-run-with` lists the tasks they can't overlap with on one agent.
Provide the constraints inline, or a path to a YAML file with a top-level `scheduling-constraints` list.

```yaml
# .nx/ci-config.yaml
dte:
  scheduling-constraints:
    # e2e tasks share a resource, so never run two of them on one agent at once
    - targets: ['e2e*']
      cannot-run-with:
        - targets: ['e2e*']
```

Tasks outside the constraint still share the agent with them.
Constraints only apply within one agent, so matching tasks still run at the same time on different agents.

{% aside type="caution" title="Constraints need continuous task assignment" %}
Nx Cloud enforces scheduling constraints only when continuous task assignment is on for the run.
Turning on [dynamic task packing](/docs/features/ci-features/distribute-task-execution#turn-on-dynamic-task-packing) also turns it on, and so does setting `NX_CLOUD_CONTINUOUS_ASSIGNMENT=true` in the main job.
Without it, Nx Cloud ignores the constraints instead of failing the run, and the pipeline's configuration in Nx Cloud shows a **Constraints not applied** warning.
{% /aside %}

#### Constraint format

| Key               | Required                                                | Value                                                                                                                             |
| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `projects`        | At least one of `projects`, `targets`, `configurations` | Globs matched against the task's project.                                                                                         |
| `targets`         | At least one of `projects`, `targets`, `configurations` | Globs matched against the task's target.                                                                                          |
| `configurations`  | At least one of `projects`, `targets`, `configurations` | Globs matched against the task's configuration. A task run without a configuration matches `default`.                             |
| `cannot-run-with` | Yes                                                     | A non-empty list of selectors. Each selector sets at least one of `projects`, `targets`, and `configurations`, and no other keys. |

The top-level keys select the tasks a constraint applies to.
While one of them runs on an agent, Nx Cloud doesn't start a task there that matches any selector in its `cannot-run-with` list, and the other way round.

Every selector key takes a non-empty list of strings.
A task matches a selector when it matches every key the selector sets, and it matches a key when it matches any glob in that key's list.
Globs use the [assignment rule glob syntax](/docs/kb/assignment-rules#glob-reference): `*`, `?`, a leading `!` to negate a glob, and comma-separated globs in one entry that must all match.

Nx Cloud also applies these rules:

- A task never conflicts with itself, so a constraint can name the same tasks on both sides, as in the example above.
- A task runs its continuous dependencies, such as the `serve` task an e2e task needs, on its own agent. Nx Cloud checks those against the constraints as well.
- Tasks matched by a constraint's top-level keys, and the tasks they depend on, are scheduled ahead of unconstrained work, so they don't queue up at the end of the run.
- Tasks whose outputs overlap never run on the same agent at the same time, even without a constraint.

#### Self-referential selectors

Inside `cannot-run-with`, a selector value can refer to the task the constraint matched.
Each token stands for that task's own value and matches tasks that share it:

| Token                   | Allowed under    | Matches tasks with the same                                 |
| ----------------------- | ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| `${self.project}`       | `projects`       | project                                                     |
| `${self.target}`        | `targets`        | target                                                      |
| `${self.configuration}` | `configurations` | configuration, where a task without one counts as `default` |

For example, the e2e tests of one project might all start a dev server on the same port, while tests of different projects use different ports:

```yaml
# .nx/ci-config.yaml
dte:
  scheduling-constraints:
    # e2e tasks of one project share its dev server port
    - targets: ['e2e-ci*']
      cannot-run-with:
        - projects: ['${self.project}']
          targets: ['e2e-ci*']
```

`app-a:e2e-ci--login.spec.ts` and `app-a:e2e-ci--cart.spec.ts` never run on one agent at the same time.
`app-a:e2e-ci--login.spec.ts` and `app-b:e2e-ci--login.spec.ts` can.

A token can sit next to other globs in the same list.
`projects: ['${self.project}', 'shared-db']` matches tasks of the same project and tasks of `shared-db`.

Nx Cloud rejects a token that breaks any of these rules:

- Use tokens only in `cannot-run-with` selectors, never in a constraint's top-level keys.
- Quote the value, and make the token the whole value. `'${self.project}'` works, while an unquoted `${self.project}` or `'app-${self.project}'` doesn't.
- Put each token only under its own key. `${self.project}` under `targets` is rejected.

#### Constraints in a separate file

Set `dte.scheduling-constraints` to a file path instead of a list.
Nx Cloud treats the value as a path when it contains a `/` or ends in `.yaml` or `.yml`, and resolves it from the workspace root.
The file wraps the list in a top-level `scheduling-constraints:` key.

```yaml
# .nx/ci-config.yaml
dte:
  scheduling-constraints: .nx/scheduling-constraints.yaml
```

```yaml
# .nx/scheduling-constraints.yaml
scheduling-constraints:
  - targets: ['e2e*']
    cannot-run-with:
      - targets: ['e2e*']
```

An empty file, or a file without the top-level `scheduling-constraints` key, fails the run.

The file that `dte.distribute-on` points at can also hold a `scheduling-constraints:` list next to `distribute-on:`.
Nx Cloud uses that list only when `.nx/ci-config.yaml` sets no `dte.scheduling-constraints` for the current environment.

An [override](#overrides) for an environment replaces the whole list.
Set it to an empty list to turn constraints off in that environment:

```yaml
# .nx/ci-config.yaml
dte:
  scheduling-constraints: .nx/scheduling-constraints.yaml
overrides:
  nightly:
    dte:
      scheduling-constraints: []
```

#### Non-parallel tasks

A target that sets `parallelism: false` in [project configuration](/docs/reference/project-configuration) runs differently depending on dynamic task packing:

- **Packing on:** the task shares its worker with other tasks. Two `parallelism: false` tasks that share a project or a target never run on the same worker at the same time.
- **Packing off:** the task runs alone. Nx Cloud assigns it only to an agent with no other work, and sends that agent nothing else until the task finishes.

With packing on, give a task an agent to itself with a constraint whose `cannot-run-with` matches every task:

```yaml
# .nx/ci-config.yaml
dte:
  scheduling-constraints:
    # The load test measures the whole machine, so nothing else may run beside it
    - projects: ['api']
      targets: ['load-test']
      cannot-run-with:
        - projects: ['*']
```

This selector also matches the task's own continuous dependencies, which must run beside it.
For a task that has them, narrow the selector, for example with `targets: ['!serve']`.

#### Validation errors

Nx Cloud checks every constraint before the run starts and lists each problem with its path, line, and column:

```plaintext
Invalid scheduling constraints:
- scheduling-constraints[0].project: unknown key; only cannot-run-with, configurations, projects, targets are accepted (unknown-key; invalid: project) at 2:5 in .nx/scheduling-constraints.yaml
```

| Code                      | Cause                                                                                                                                          |
| ------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `unknown-key`             | A key other than the four above, such as `project` or `target` in the singular. Selectors in `cannot-run-with` don't accept `cannot-run-with`. |
| `duplicate-key`           | The same key twice in one mapping, including a second top-level `scheduling-constraints` key.                                                  |
| `missing-selector`        | A constraint or a `cannot-run-with` selector without `projects`, `targets`, or `configurations`.                                               |
| `missing-cannot-run-with` | A constraint without `cannot-run-with`, or with an empty list.                                                                                 |
| `empty-selector`          | A selector key set to an empty list, such as `targets: []`.                                                                                    |
| `invalid-node-type`       | A value of the wrong shape, such as `targets: e2e` instead of `targets: ['e2e']`.                                                              |
| `non-string-selector`     | A selector value YAML doesn't read as a string, such as `42` or `true`. Quote it.                                                              |
| `invalid-self-token`      | A self-referential token that breaks one of the [token rules](#self-referential-selectors).                                                    |

A file that isn't valid YAML, isn't a mapping, or is missing its top-level key fails with `Invalid scheduling constraints YAML` and the reason.

A constraint can also contradict the task graph.
A task and the continuous tasks it depends on have to run on one agent, so a run fails when a constraint keeps them apart:

```plaintext
Scheduling constraints contradict required continuous-task overlap (1 conflict(s)): ...
```

The message names each pair of conflicting tasks and the dependency chain that forces them together.
Narrow the constraint so it no longer matches both tasks.

### `dte.assignment-rules`

Rules that pin matching tasks to specific agent types.
Provide the rules inline, or a path to a YAML file containing them.
See [assignment rules](/docs/kb/assignment-rules) for the rule format.

```yaml
# .nx/ci-config.yaml
dte:
  assignment-rules: .nx/assignment-rules.yaml
```

```yaml
# .nx/ci-config.yaml
dte:
  assignment-rules:
    - projects:
        - '*'
      targets:
        - build
      run-on:
        - agent: linux-large-A
          parallelism: 2
```

Each inline rule matches tasks by `projects`, `targets`, and `configurations` globs, and routes them to the agents listed under `run-on`.
`run-on` is required on every rule. Each `run-on` entry needs an `agent`, with optional `parallelism`.
With [dynamic task packing](/docs/features/ci-features/distribute-task-execution#turn-on-dynamic-task-packing) on, `parallelism` is ignored and each agent takes as many tasks as fit its CPU and memory.

## `workers`

Configuration for the Workers.
The older `nx-agents` key still works, and its entries merge with `workers`.

```yaml
# .nx/ci-config.yaml
workers:
  with-env-vars:
    - CHROMATIC_BRANCH
    - CHROMATIC_SHA
```

### `workers.with-env-vars`

Environment variables forwarded from the main job to every Worker.
Variables prefixed with `NX_` are forwarded automatically, so list only the additional ones you need.

Type: array of strings.

## `overrides`

Per-environment overrides keyed by [`NX_CI_EXECUTION_ENV`](/docs/reference/environment-variables) value.
Each override is a partial config that takes precedence over the base sections when its key matches the active environment.
This has no `start-ci-run` flag equivalent. It is specific to the config file.

```yaml
# .nx/ci-config.yaml
dte:
  distribute-on: 5 linux-large
  assignment-rules:
    - targets:
        - e2e-ci
      run-on:
        - agent: linux-large
          parallelism: 1

overrides:
  main:
    dte:
      assignment-rules:
        - targets:
            - e2e-ci
          run-on:
            - agent: linux-extra-large
              parallelism: 1
```

In this example every environment distributes across `linux-large` agents and routes `e2e-ci` tasks to a `linux-large` agent.
When `NX_CI_EXECUTION_ENV` is `main`, the override replaces `assignment-rules`, routing `e2e-ci` to a `linux-extra-large` agent instead.

Overrides apply per key. An override replaces the base value for the keys it sets, so an array like `assignment-rules` is replaced as a whole rather than merged with the base rules.
Keys the override does not set keep their base values, so `distribute-on` remains `5 linux-large` on `main`.

An override only replaces the sections you set. Sections you leave out fall back to the base config, and the base config falls back to defaults.
